  "body": "**Requested by the user, 2026-11-12 (filed by an agent). The maintainer reviews the scope before any work starts.** It carries `needs-research`, so Pick never takes it for code. Once the questions below are settled, it gets the usual concrete sub-issues.\n\n## The idea\nA post option, **Explain G-code** (yes/no, default **no**), that adds a short comment to every block saying what it does. For example:\n\n```\nG20 G17 G90 G91.1 G94 G40 G49 G80 (INCH, XY PLANE, ABSOLUTE, ARC CENTERS INCREMENTAL, FEED PER MINUTE, CUTTER COMP OFF, TOOL LENGTH OFF, CYCLES OFF)\nG64 P0.001 (PATH BLENDING, 0.001 IN TOLERANCE)\nG99 (CANNED CYCLE RETURNS TO R PLANE)\nG73 X0.7874 Y0.3937 Z-0.9449 R0 Q0.7087 F3.94 (CHIP-BREAK PECK DRILL TO Z-0.9449, 0.7087 PECKS)\nG53 G0 Z0 (RAPID TO MACHINE Z HOME)\nM30 (END OF PROGRAM, REWIND)\n```\n\nIt helps users learning G-code, makes a posted program easier to check by eye before it runs (the Hub reviews showed how useful that is), and helps when someone hand-edits a program at the control.\n\n## Open questions for the maintainer\n1. **Where the option lives:** a per-post property (each `.cps` declares it), a machine-file setting, a document/setup post setting, or all of these with an order of precedence? Is it also in the post dialog and over MCP?\n2. **Scope:** every post, or the Tormach/LinuxCNC family first? Each dialect has its own comment syntax and limits: `( )` versus `;`, Fanuc-family line and comment length limits, controls that drop or reject long comments, and Heidenhain conversational has no G words at all.\n3. **Safety:** comments must stay inert. On PathPilot/LinuxCNC a comment that begins with MSG, DEBUG, PRINT, LOG, PY, etc. is executed, and the existing `note()` guard has to cover these too. No comment may change program behaviour, and a golden should check that a program with and without the option gives byte-identical motion once comments are stripped.\n4. **Wording source:** the comment text must be original, written from the public G-code references (RS-274/ISO 6983, LinuxCNC docs, and the control makers' public manuals), never copied from another product's output.\n5. **Size:** a much longer program file on controls with little memory. Should the post warn, or refuse, on a machine file that states a program size limit?\n6. **Detail level:** one comment per block, or a modal-change-only mode that comments a word only when it changes?\n\n## Done means (for this umbrella)\nThe questions are answered in comments and the sub-issues are filed, each with `P*`, `effort:*` and `area:*`. Then this umbrella is closed.\n",
